# PROJECT: BUILD A COMPLETE AGENCY MANAGEMENT CRM — AGENCY COMMAND CENTER

Act as a senior full-stack software engineer, database architect, UI/UX designer, product manager, and security engineer.

Your task is to design and build a complete, production-oriented web application called **Agency Command Center**.

This is a private business management platform for a web development and digital services agency. The agency finds businesses that need websites, contacts potential clients through outreachers, closes website development deals, assigns projects to developers, tracks revenue and expenses, and manages its entire workflow.

I do NOT want a static website, a landing page, a simple dashboard mockup, or a UI prototype with non-functional buttons.

I want a fully functional application with a real database, persistent data storage, working forms, working filters, functional navigation, team management, location management, client management, project management, and downloadable spreadsheets.

Every important button, form, search box, filter, table action, and navigation item must work.

Do not use fake functionality. Do not create buttons that only display alerts or pretend that an operation succeeded without actually saving the data.

Build the application systematically, starting with the application architecture and core functionality, then implementing each module and connecting it to the database.

If a feature requires external credentials, API keys, or configuration, create the integration structure and clearly document what must be configured. Do not pretend an unconfigured integration works.

==================================================
1. APPLICATION OBJECTIVE
==================================================

The application must solve five major problems.

1. Store and organize countries, states, provinces, districts, counties, cities, towns, and localities.
2. Allow me to select and save locations so I never need to search for the same location repeatedly.
3. Manage all business leads and clients in a centralized CRM.
4. Manage outreachers who contact prospects and sell websites.
5. Manage website developers, client projects, deadlines, payments, and profitability.

The application must also support spreadsheet imports and exports.

All modules must be connected.

Example workflow:

I select India → Karnataka → Bengaluru Urban → Bengaluru → Whitefield.

I save Whitefield as a target location and select Jewellery Stores as the business category.

I import or manually add jewellery stores from Whitefield to my CRM.

I assign selected leads to an outreacher.

The outreacher contacts the businesses and records the results.

When a business agrees to purchase a website, I mark it as a won deal.

I assign the project to a developer.

The developer updates the project status until the website is completed and delivered.

The application calculates the project's financial results and updates the dashboard.

This entire workflow must work using persistent database records.

